The bug is that the goroutine closure captures the loop variable `i` by reference, not by value. In Go 1.21, loop variables are shared across all iterations of the loop, so by the time the goroutines run, `i` has already been incremented to 5.

To fix this, you need to capture the current value of `i` in a new variable inside the loop, or pass it as a parameter to the goroutine function.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

